## Claim
Text: "A man who received four ballot applications votes four times in the 2020 election."
Claim author: James Snapp.
Claim date: October 16, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did any news outlet or official source report on a specific man receiving four ballot applications and voting four times in the 2020 election?
Answer: The search result, which is an official North Carolina State Board of Elections page describing provisional voting procedures, does not contain any report of a specific man receiving four ballot applications and voting four times in the 2020 election. No such incident is mentioned.

Source URL: https://www.ncsbe.gov/voting/provisional-voting

### Was the man who allegedly voted four times in the 2020 election ever charged with voter fraud or any related crime?
Answer: Based solely on the provided search result, there is no information indicating that Ralph Shicatano, the man who voted in person after requesting a mail-in ballot, was ever charged with voter fraud or any related crime. The article notes that Shicatano "said he’s done nothing wrong" and that he "did not vote by mail," and it does not mention any charges being filed against him.

Source URL: https://www.penncapital-star.com/government-politics/trump-campaign-cites-republican-candidate-proof-of-mail-in-voter-fraud-in-pennsylvania/

### Did the man whose name is associated with this claim actually vote in multiple locations or with multiple ballots in the 2020 election, as verified by voter rolls?
Answer: No, the man associated with the claim, Ralph Shicatano, did not actually vote multiple times in the 2020 election. According to the source, Shicatano requested a mail-in ballot but decided to vote in person; he did not submit the mail-in ballot and only voted once using a machine at the polls. The article notes that "Shicatano did not vote by mail, and said he still has his incomplete mail-in ballot," and there is no verified evidence from voter rolls that he cast more than one vote.

Source URL: https://www.penncapital-star.com/government-politics/trump-campaign-cites-republican-candidate-proof-of-mail-in-voter-fraud-in-pennsylvania/

## Final Judgement
Thekey insight from the fact-check is that the claim refers to a specific man, Ralph Shicatano, who requested a mail-in ballot but voted in person without submitting the mail-in ballot, and there is no evidence he voted multiple times. The source explicitly states he "did not vote by mail" and only voted once using a machine. No mention of four ballot applications or four votes exists in the evidence. The claim is directly contradicted by the recorded information.

